A||||11||Firebug||2.8 Blocks for 3.1 conversions||||1||
Z||000000||Firebug||02-22-2001||04:42 PM||||I know that I'm probably beating an issue to death that's probably been covered over and over, but I would like to turn my fiero into a v-6 car and I would like to know if certain year 2.8 blocks make better candidates than others for 3.1 conversions.  Are there 2.8L blocks I should look for?  Are there certain 3.1 cranks I should look for?  Year and models would be very much appreciated.  Thank you very much.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||02-22-2001||07:19 PM||||If you dont have a 2.8-liter engine than just get the 3.1-liter engine with the iron heads and you're all set. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||02-22-2001||10:09 PM||||If you read this ad it seems all 2.8 will work. Has anyone used this kit? It seems reasonable in cost. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/pisa4.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/pisa4.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||02-23-2001||04:16 PM||||The best 2.8 block for a 3.1 conversion is a 3.4 Block  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  If you are going from a Duke to a v6, there is no reason to stop at 3.1L.  get a low mileage 92-95 3.4L Camaro engine for the conversion.  It wouldn't be hard to find a tired 2.8 that you could strip intake and accessories off of.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||02-23-2001||07:17 PM||||Mark, did you get your block drilled?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shaun41178||02-24-2001||06:28 PM||||i have been trying to get rid of a rotating assembly like what pisa is selling in that link for some time now at a much cheaper price and i still get people trying to talk me down.<P>The only thing that I don't have that the pisa kit has is the bearings, the timing chain and the FPR.  But those parts only add up to about $150 anyways.  But one thing I have that this Pisa doesn't have is that mine is balanced and ready to go.  Balancing will cost another $200.<P>Check the Mall section for my post but i am asking $600.  Go there for more details<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||02-25-2001||11:47 AM||||just make sure the 2.8 is for a Fiero, not another GM car or truck, starter bolt holes are on the wrong side, youde have to have new ones drilled, while the engine is out.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Firebug||02-25-2001||01:05 PM||||I've looked at all of your advice and it seems like my best option would be to get a 3.1 out of a beretta and make sure I get the flywheel with it.  Then remove the aluminum heads and install my fiero 2.8 heads.  I would also have to get pistons for a '91 Lumina APV with a 3.1 with cast iron heads.  I rebuild engines, so this isn't a problem.  I just wasn't sure of the parts sources.  Do I sound right???<P>Thanks you for your help.<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000008||fieroshop||02-25-2001||02:27 PM||||A 3.1 engine with aluminum heads will not work. If you replace the heads with cast iron heads, your compression will be too low and you will also have to replace the pistons as well. <P>------------------<BR> <BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shaun41178||02-25-2001||04:29 PM||||Since you said you rebuild engines then its not that hard to do.  yea get the Beretta engine with the aluminum heads, take those heads off, replace the pistons with iron head pistons when you rebuild it and then throw on iron heads and you will be all set.  The only difference between the aluminum head engines and the Fiero engines is the heads, intake and pistons.  The crank too of course but that is a given in a 2.8-3.1 application.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mshill||02-26-2001||12:57 PM||||terryk,<P>I borrowed (rented) a jig from a forum member and did the drilling/tapping myself.  Turned out to be a one hour job and very easy to do with the jig.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ThatRickGuy||02-27-2001||09:44 AM||||I did the 2.8 to 3.2 conversion over fall. And while I love the extra power, for the money, it would have been as easy to pick up the 3.4 crate for $1700. Considering I blew right arround $2k for the complete rebuild, blue printed, valve job, everything decked. Then tacked on a new starter, battery, and a slew of other add on parts that would have needed replacing anyways. ||||reg||1
